Bruce schrieb:
> 
> In rec.games.roguelike.nethack nyra <nyra@gmx.net> wrote:
> > I'm interested what the _actual_ justification for omitting the "i" in
> > aluminium is, which appears in every language i'm aware of _except for
> > U.S. english_.
> 
> The person who named it, Sir Humphry Davy, originally called it
> "alumium", then "aluminum".  The British took it upon themselves 4 years
> later to amend that to "aluminium".

Thanks for the details, i was able to find some pretty good info with
what you provided. 

> Rather, the British spelling _adds_ the 'i'.

The sites i found state that "aluminium" was the accepted spelling in
the U.S., too, until the U.S. chemical society decided to _revert_ to
"aluminum" in 1925 - 100th anniversary of the first elemental
alumin[i]um produced?

> > "Aluminium" is derived from "alumen", and just like in "Sodium"
> > (derived from "soda"[1]) and "Potassium" (derived from "potash"), an
> > "i" is placed between the "stem" word and the "-um". It looks rather
> > arbitrary to me, but "aluminum" is no less arbitrary.
> 
> I think that's exactly it.  It's arbitrary.

I agree. And given the history of the name, "aluminum" can claim
seniority; "aluminium" fits better with other elements' names (a _bit_
better, only; there are several english element names ending in -um,
not -ium, as you showed [but there's not a single one in german, which
triggered my "aluminum sounds wrong" reflex]).

